<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ CAN A HOST REGISTER ITS OWN `<send>` / `<invoke>` TYPE?

  Register item 470's SECOND stage rests entirely on this. The first stage moved
  one decision into the document; the second would move the ACTS there too, by
  letting the document say `<send type="sprag">` and having this crate's driver
  be what carries it out. If a host cannot introduce a type, that design is not
  available at the pinned engine and the driver stays the place acts live.

  ⚠⚠⚠ ASKED BY COMPILING AND RUNNING, because everything else about it is a
  fact about somewhere else. W3C SCXML 6.2 says a `type` the platform does not
  support raises `error.execution`, and a conforming engine is therefore ALLOWED
  to support more; SCE's own tree carries `sce:mesh-rpc` as a type its codegen
  knows, which proves the set can hold more than the standard two and says
  nothing about whether a CONSUMER can add to it.

  ⚠⚠ A NO IS THE USEFUL ANSWER TOO, and this file is written expecting one: it
  is the difference between item 470 stage 2 being a design and being a request
  filed upstream. What it must not do is leave the question answered by reading.

  This document is deliberately SMALL and its states are stable: what is being
  measured is what the engine does at ONE entry, not a topology.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_send_type"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="sending">

  <datamodel>
    <!-- How many `error.execution` events arrived. W3C SCXML 6.2's answer for a
         type the platform does not support. -->
    <data id="errors" expr="0"/>
    <!-- Whether the event a custom typed `<send>` names ever came back. If a
         host could register the type and did not, this stays 0 and `errors`
         rises; if the engine ignored the type and delivered it internally, this
         rises and the type meant nothing. The two are different answers. -->
    <data id="landed" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE CONTROL, AND WITHOUT IT THIS DOCUMENT PROVES NOTHING. A
         `landed` of zero has two readings: the typed send was refused, or
         `<send>` does not deliver in this document at all. This is a send with
         NO type in the SAME state and the same entry, so it separates them. -->
    <data id="plain" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ IN WHICH ORDER TWO EVENTS FROM ONE HOST ACT ARRIVED. SCE widened a
      handler's reply from ONE event to a LIST at rev 084dfdbf, and the whole of
      what a list adds over a single reply is the ORDER — so two counters would
      measure nothing, because both would read 1 whichever way round they came.

      ⚠⚠ RANKS RATHER THAN A STRING, and the reason is the engine underneath: the
      datamodel is declared `ecmascript` while the IScriptEngine supplied here is
      Lua, so `seen + '1'` means two different things in the two languages and a
      concatenated trace would be measuring the script engine. Arithmetic on a
      counter is the one spelling that is identical in both.
    -->
    <data id="ticks" expr="0"/>
    <data id="rank1" expr="0"/>
    <data id="rank2" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ CAN A **TRANSITION** ASK A HOST FOR AN ACT, THE WAY A STATE ENTRY
      CAN? Register item 470's stage 2 finished every `<onentry>` prompt this
      loop has and stopped at exactly one arm: `prompt.turn` sits on three of
      the four EDGES into `working`, so the arrival state cannot say what is
      owed and no state entry can declare it. Whether the same act can be
      declared where the act actually belongs is UNMEASURED, and everything
      after stage 2 turns on it.

      ⚠⚠ THE VALUE IS COMPOSED BY THE DOCUMENT so that a handler reading it
      back has read an EXPRESSION evaluated at send time, not a literal the
      test could have written itself. It is deliberately a sentence no Rust in
      this crate spells.
    -->
    <data id="carried" expr="'only this document could have composed this'"/>
    <!-- The CONTROL's arrival: the same act, the same argument, declared on a
         state ENTRY. Without it, a zero below reads both as *a transition
         cannot* and as *this document cannot*, which are different answers. -->
    <data id="entry_landed" expr="0"/>
    <!-- The QUESTION's arrival: the same act declared on a TARGETED transition,
         which is the shape `prompt.turn` would have to take. -->
    <data id="edge_landed" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ AND THE THIRD SHAPE, WHICH IS WHAT REGISTER ITEM 470's STAGE 3
      RESTS ON: the same act declared on a TARGETLESS transition — W3C SCXML
      3.13's transition that runs its content and changes no state.

      Stage 3 has to move `pump`'s twenty-eight-arm state match into the
      document, and a state ENTRY cannot carry it: `working` is pumped many
      times over one entry, so what a pass does has to be answerable on every
      pass rather than once. A targetless transition is the only shape that
      answers a driver's question without moving the machine and without
      re-running an `<onentry>` that would re-type a prompt.

      ⚠ It is measured AFTER the targeted edge above and in the same run, so
      the harder shape is this one's control rather than the other way round.
    -->
    <data id="standing_landed" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ HOW MANY TIMES `edged` HAS BEEN ENTERED, and it is the control the
      question above cannot do without. *The machine did not move* is not the
      claim — a SELF transition also leaves it where it was, and would re-run
      every `<onentry>` on the way. Comparing the current state before and
      after is blind to exactly that, so what is counted is the ENTRY.
    -->
    <data id="entered" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A NUMBER LOOKS LIKE WHEN IT CROSSES AS A `<param>`.

      Register item 470's stage 2 has three datamodel back doors left, and every
      one of them reads a NUMBER out of the script session with a typed getter:
      `await_person_ms`, `handback_still_ms`, `ready_timeout_ms`. Their meaning
      turns on distinctions a typed read makes and a string might not:

        * ZERO is a real answer and not an absence. `await_person_ms = 0` means
          NOBODY IS EXPECTED, and `handback_still_ms = 0` means a person who
          takes the pane KEEPS it. Both are decisions the file is entitled to
          state, and both would be destroyed by a reader that treated 0 as nil.
        * NIL is a different real answer: the document declares no bound.
        * A NEGATIVE number is refused today, so it must stay tellable.

      A host act's arguments arrive as strings. Whether those three stay
      distinguishable across that crossing is what decides if these doors can be
      shut the way `service_needle` was, and it is UNMEASURED.
    -->
    <data id="counted" expr="42"/>
    <data id="zeroed" expr="0"/>
    <data id="negated" expr="-1"/>
    <!-- ⚠ NO `expr`, so this is nil in the datamodel's own language. -->
    <data id="blanked"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ WHAT A LIST OF OBJECTS LOOKS LIKE WHEN IT CROSSES AS A `<param>`.

      Register item 470's stage 2 has ONE datamodel back door left, and the
      answer above does not reach it. `consenting()` reads `may_answer`, which
      is not a number: it is an ARRAY OF OBJECTS, each `{asked, answer}`, and
      `OuterLoop::consents_in` walks it with typed getters two levels deep. A
      host act's arguments arrive as `HashMap<String, Vec<String>>`, so the
      question is what a structure becomes on the way across, and it has three
      candidate answers this file measures rather than reasons about:

        * IT SERIALISES into something a driver could parse back.
        * IT FLATTENS into a handle with no content (`table: 0x...`), an empty
          string, or nothing at all, which shuts the road for this shape.
        * INDEXING works, so a document could carry the parts separately.

      ⚠⚠ AND A FOURTH QUESTION BESIDE THEM, which is the fallback that does not
      depend on serialisation at all: SCE's params map keeps a VECTOR per name,
      so a repeated `<param name="x">` may arrive as several values in the
      order the document wrote them. If it does, a fixed number of clause slots
      can cross without any structure crossing.

      ⚠ NO `expr` HERE. The value is PLANTED BY THE GATE, as the exact
      `ScriptValue::Array(Object)` shape `consents_in` reads, so what is
      measured is the crossing rather than how this engine parses an
      ECMAScript array literal it is not written in. See `edged`'s
      `probe.list` and `probe.deep`.
    -->
    <data id="clauses"/>
    <!-- ⚠ THE CONTROL FOR THE REPEATED NAME: a plain string, so a `twice` that
         arrives with one value can be told from one that arrives with none. -->
    <data id="clause_word" expr="'plain'"/>
  </datamodel>

  <!--
    ⚠ THE TRANSITIONS HERE ARE TARGETLESS ON PURPOSE. W3C SCXML 3.13: a
    transition with no `target` runs its content and changes no state, so
    recording an arrival cannot re-enter this state and re-run the `<send>` in
    its `onentry`. A self transition would have made this document a loop that
    sends forever.
  -->
  <state id="sending">
    <onentry>
      <!-- ⚠ The control: same state, same entry, and the type W3C SCXML 6.2
           NAMES as the one every engine supports. Comparing against a send with
           no `type` at all was the first attempt and it was the wrong control,
           because it does not distinguish *this type is unsupported* from
           *`<send>` does nothing here*. This one does. -->
      <send type="http://www.w3.org/TR/scxml/#SCXMLEventProcessor"
            event="reached.plain"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE TYPED SEND IS LAST, AND THE ORDER IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER
        THAN A STYLE. With it FIRST the control never ran at all: an error in
        executable content abandons the REST OF THE BLOCK, so `plain` stayed 0
        and the single `error.execution` could have come from either send. The
        first version of this file read that as *the control does not deliver*
        and was one edit away from recording the wrong finding.
      -->
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="reached.host"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="error.execution">
      <assign location="errors" expr="errors + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="reached.host">
      <assign location="landed" expr="landed + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="reached.plain">
      <assign location="plain" expr="plain + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TWO EVENTS ONE HOST ACT MAY NOW PRODUCE, and the names share no
      prefix with `reached.*` on purpose: an SCXML event descriptor matches by
      PREFIX, so calling these `reached.one` / `reached.two` would put them in the
      same family as the control and the host measurement above. This repository
      has already paid for a sibling swallowing a whole compound.

      ⚠⚠ NOTHING HERE FIRES IN THE OTHER PROBES OVER THIS DOCUMENT. Their handler
      answers the request's own `reached.host`, so these two stay at 0 and the
      counters they read are untouched — which is what makes this addition safe to
      make to a document three other gates already measure.
    -->
    <transition event="ordered.one">
      <assign location="ticks" expr="ticks + 1"/>
      <assign location="rank1" expr="ticks"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="ordered.two">
      <assign location="ticks" expr="ticks + 1"/>
      <assign location="rank2" expr="ticks"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="go" target="invoking"/>
    <!-- ⚠ CARRIES NOTHING, deliberately: this edge only puts the machine where
         the two measurements below are made, so nothing it does can be mistaken
         for what they measure. -->
    <transition event="probe.enter" target="edging"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE CONTROL AND THE QUESTION, ONE ACT APART.

    `edging`'s ENTRY declares a host act with an argument; its EDGE out declares
    the same act with the same argument. One handler sees both, in one run, in
    that order. So a `edge_landed` of zero cannot be read as *arguments do not
    cross this boundary at all* while `entry_landed` is one, and a pair of zeros
    says the handler was never reached rather than that a transition is special.

    ⚠⚠ THE EDGE HAS A TARGET, which is the shape being asked about rather than
    a convenience. `prompt.turn` sits on `judging -> working`, and W3C SCXML
    3.13 runs a transition's executable content BETWEEN exiting the source and
    entering the target — a moment no state's entry can stand in for. A
    targetless transition would have measured an easier question.

    ⚠⚠⚠ A NO IS THE USEFUL ANSWER TOO, and it has three shapes this document
    keeps apart: the send is REFUSED (`errors` rises), the send reaches the host
    with NO arguments (`edge_landed` rises and the handler saw an empty bag),
    or the event is delivered internally and the type meant nothing (the reply
    arrives without the handler having been called). The gate reading this
    document is what tells the last one from the first.
  -->
  <state id="edging">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.entry">
        <param name="word" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="carried.entry">
      <assign location="entry_landed" expr="entry_landed + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="error.execution">
      <assign location="errors" expr="errors + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE QUESTION ITSELF. If this reaches the host carrying `word`, then
      `prompt.turn` can leave the driver by the same road the five acts before
      it took, and `Owed::on` can be deleted rather than emptied.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.take" target="edged">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.edge">
        <param name="word" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠ WHERE THE REPLY IS HEARD. The handler's answer is queued and delivered
    after the transition completes, so the machine is already here by the time
    it arrives; a document that listened for it back in `edging` would read a
    zero and call the road shut.
  -->
  <state id="edged">
    <!-- ⚠ See `entered`: this is what tells a targetless transition from a
         self transition, which the machine's current state cannot. -->
    <onentry>
      <assign location="entered" expr="entered + 1"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="carried.edge">
      <assign location="edge_landed" expr="edge_landed + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="error.execution">
      <assign location="errors" expr="errors + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE THIRD QUESTION: a TARGETLESS transition declaring the same act
      with the same argument. If this reaches the host carrying `word`, then a
      driver can ASK its document what a pass is for and be answered without
      the machine moving — which is the road register item 470's stage 3 needs
      and the one neither shape above stands in for.

      ⚠⚠ THE NAMES SHARE NO SECOND TOKEN with `carried.entry` or `carried.edge`.
      An SCXML event descriptor matches by prefix, and a sibling swallowing a
      whole compound is a defect this repository has already paid for.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.stand">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.standing">
        <param name="word" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <transition event="carried.standing">
      <assign location="standing_landed" expr="standing_landed + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE FIFTH QUESTION: can a NUMBER cross, and do zero, negative and
      nil stay tellable apart? See the `<data>` block above for why the answer
      decides whether item 470's last three datamodel back doors can be shut.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.count">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.count">
        <param name="counted" expr="counted"/>
        <param name="zeroed" expr="zeroed"/>
        <param name="negated" expr="negated"/>
        <param name="blanked" expr="blanked"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE SIXTH QUESTION: can a LIST OF OBJECTS cross, and if not, can a
      REPEATED NAME carry the parts? See the `clauses` entry in the datamodel
      above for why the answer decides item 470's last back door.

      ⚠⚠ TWO SENDS RATHER THAN ONE, AND THAT IS ISOLATION RATHER THAN TIDINESS.
      The indexing expressions below could RAISE — this datamodel is declared
      `ecmascript` and the engine underneath is Lua, whose tables are 1 based —
      and a raise while evaluating one `<param>` could take the whole send with
      it. Asked in the same send, a failure there would read as *a list cannot
      cross*, which is a different answer entirely. ⚠ MEASURED 2026-08-26: the
      raise did not happen and this pairing indexes from ZERO, so the isolation
      bought nothing this time. It stays, because what it insures against is
      the next question asked here rather than this one.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.list">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.list">
        <param name="listed" expr="clauses"/>
        <!-- ⚠ THE CONTROL: a plain string in the SAME send, so `listed`
             arriving empty can be told from the send never arriving. -->
        <param name="word" expr="clause_word"/>
        <!-- ⚠⚠ THE SAME NAME TWICE, WITH TWO DIFFERENT VALUES. If both arrive
             in this order, a document can carry N clause slots without any
             structure crossing at all. -->
        <param name="twice" expr="clause_word"/>
        <param name="twice" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠ BOTH BASES ARE ASKED FOR so the answer is not confounded by which one
      this pairing indexes from, and NEITHER indexes through a value that could
      be nil: the gate plants TWO clauses, so `clauses[1]` holds an object
      whichever way round the base is. `clauses[0].asked` was not asked for
      because under a 1 based table it would index into nil, which raises
      rather than answering. ⚠ MEASURED 2026-08-26: `clauses[0]` is the FIRST
      element, so this pairing is 0 based and that expression would have been
      safe. The gate reads the two bases as DIFFERENT rather than pinning
      which is which, because that is the engine's to choose.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.deep">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.deep">
        <param name="at_zero" expr="clauses[0]"/>
        <param name="at_one" expr="clauses[1]"/>
        <param name="field" expr="clauses[1].asked"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE EDGE CARRIES THE CONTROL, and it has to be HERE rather than
      anywhere earlier. The question below is whether a FINAL can ask; a
      control that fires in an earlier macrostep would only say the handler
      once worked. This one is declared on a TARGETED transition, the shape
      proven green two measurements above, and it is executed in the SAME
      macrostep that enters the ending. So if the handler stops being called
      at all in this regime, the control goes with the question, and if only
      the question goes, finals are what cannot ask.
    -->
    <transition event="probe.end" target="ended">
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.leaving">
        <param name="word" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </transition>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠⚠⚠ THE FOURTH QUESTION, AND REGISTER ITEM 470's STAGE 3 TURNS ON IT:
    can a `<final>` declare a host act on its ENTRY, and does it reach the host
    carrying its argument?

    `AiLoop::ended` maps each of this loop's seven endings to a `Verdict` with a
    28 arm state match, and that is one of the topology copies stage 3 exists to
    remove. The only road that does not put the answer back in Rust is the
    document declaring, at the ending itself, which word it publishes.

    ⚠⚠ AN ENDING IS NOT AN ORDINARY ENTRY AND THAT IS THE WHOLE QUESTION.
    Entering a top level `<final>` COMPLETES the machine, so anything the engine
    does after the entry block is a different regime from every act this crate
    already ships: no further macrostep is owed, the reply event has nowhere to
    be delivered, and an engine that tears the session down on completion would
    drop the send on the floor.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE DOCUMENT CANNOT BE THE WITNESS HERE, and that is a property of the
    question rather than a gap in this file. Every other measurement in this
    document reads a counter the document itself keeps, which needs a
    transition to hear the reply; a completed machine has no transitions left.
    So the gate over THIS one reads the handler's own record, and the reply is
    expected to go nowhere. What is being asked is only whether the HANDLER IS
    CALLED, with its argument, at all.
  -->
  <final id="ended">
    <onentry>
      <send type="x-sprag-host" event="carried.final">
        <param name="word" expr="carried"/>
      </send>
    </onentry>
  </final>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠ THE OTHER HALF, and it is a separate question rather than the same one
    twice. `<send>` picks an Event I/O Processor and `<invoke>` picks an
    invoker; SCE's codegen has a path for `sce:mesh-rpc` on the invoke side and
    `BasicHTTPEventProcessor` on the send side, which are two different
    registries. A host might be able to reach one and not the other.
  -->
  <state id="invoking">
    <invoke type="x-sprag-host"/>
    <transition event="error.execution">
      <assign location="errors" expr="errors + 1"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

</scxml>
